# `actuarial::Cashflow::horizon`

[Back to `actuarial::Cashflow`](../Cashflow.md)

The number of years the cashflow spans.

## Signature

```cpp
std::int64_t actuarial::Cashflow::horizon() const noexcept;
```

## What It Does

The number of years `n`: the death payments have `n` entries and the survival payments `n + 1`. A valuation requires the horizon not to exceed the mortality horizon of its basis, and values a shorter cashflow over its own years. An undefined cashflow has horizon 0.

## Returns

The horizon in years, 0 for an undefined or zero-year cashflow.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: the horizon of each factory

The horizon is the `years` argument, or one less than the length of the survival schedule for `make`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln("annuity(5):", actuarial::cashflow::annuity(5).horizon(), " death(3):", actuarial::cashflow::death(3).horizon(), " endowment(7):", actuarial::cashflow::endowment(7).horizon());
  out.writeln("make with 4 survival and 3 death payments:", actuarial::cashflow::make(pluss::make_series({0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0}), pluss::make_series({1.0, 1.0, 1.0})).horizon());
}
```

### Example 2: against the mortality horizon

A cashflow longer than the mortality of the basis is refused; a shorter one is valued over its own years.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  const pluss::Series qx = pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015, 0.019, 0.024});
  const actuarial::Mortality mortality = actuarial::mortality::from_qx(qx);
  const actuarial::Basis basis{mortality, actuarial::discount::flat(0.04)};
  const actuarial::Valuation value = actuarial::operators::value(basis);
  out.writeln("mortality horizon", mortality.horizon(), " cashflow horizon", actuarial::cashflow::annuity(3).horizon(), " value", value(actuarial::cashflow::annuity(3)).item<double>());
  report([&] { (void)value(actuarial::cashflow::annuity(8)); });
}
```

### Example 3: empty cashflows

An undefined cashflow and a zero-year cashflow both have horizon 0.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln("undefined:", actuarial::Cashflow{}.horizon(), " annuity(0):", actuarial::cashflow::annuity(0).horizon());
}
```

